1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,400
In warfare, the demanding and deadly challenge of extreme

2
00:00:07,401 --> 00:00:11,040
ballistic accuracy falls on the shoulders of the sniper.

3
00:00:13,160 --> 00:00:16,620
His mission, pure and simple, is to hit the bullseye.

4
00:00:19,980 --> 00:00:20,820
Sniper ready?

5
00:00:21,300 --> 00:00:22,000
Send it.

6
00:00:24,060 --> 00:00:26,840
Trainees learn the art, and experts hone it.

7
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:27,620
Good to go.

8
00:00:27,621 --> 00:00:33,220
at the U.S. Army Sniper School at Fort Benning, Georgia.

9
00:00:33,960 --> 00:00:37,260
One of the most devastatingly accurate rifles they work with

10
00:00:37,261 --> 00:00:40,960
requires a two-man team to operate effectively.

11
00:00:42,340 --> 00:00:44,300
The Barrett M107.

12
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:49,440
The M107 fires magazines of 10 .50 caliber rounds

13
00:00:49,441 --> 00:00:52,700
to cushion the recoil and increase accuracy.

14
00:00:53,560 --> 00:00:57,180
The barrel assembly itself absorbs force as each bullet exits.

15
00:00:57,320 --> 00:01:00,780
moving inward against large springs built into the receiver,

16
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:03,400
the central mount that holds the firing mechanism.

17
00:01:04,420 --> 00:01:08,960
Each round speeds toward the target at 2,850 feet per second.

18
00:01:09,960 --> 00:01:13,680
In this exercise, the challenge for Sergeant Anthony Palkin

19
00:01:13,681 --> 00:01:15,540
and Staff Sergeant Rodney Medley

20
00:01:15,541 --> 00:01:20,320
is to hit three separate bullseyes from a range of over 400 yards.

21
00:01:22,780 --> 00:01:25,680
All right, give me a left half minute.

22
00:01:25,681 --> 00:01:28,520
Medley, the spotter, uses a more powerful scope

23
00:01:28,620 --> 00:01:31,620
to determine the precise coordinates.

24
00:01:31,720 --> 00:01:34,520
Palky, the sniper, must adjust to those coordinates.

25
00:01:34,620 --> 00:01:35,660
Left, half a minute.

26
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:38,160
Send one more.

27
00:01:38,260 --> 00:01:41,160
To have a chance of hitting the bullseye.

28
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:44,360
The M107's accuracy has one crucial variable working

29
00:01:44,460 --> 00:01:48,640
against it at Fort Benning, the wind.

30
00:01:48,740 --> 00:01:50,680
The wind conditions are real rough.

31
00:01:50,780 --> 00:01:53,600
From 300 to 500 meters, it's all open from here

32
00:01:53,700 --> 00:01:54,940
to 500 meters out.

33
00:01:54,941 --> 00:01:57,440
So you've got the wind that just pushes from the right to left.

34
00:01:57,441 --> 00:01:59,240
All right, 12 o'clock, 4 inches.

35
00:01:59,241 --> 00:02:00,440
Send another.

36
00:02:00,441 --> 00:02:02,140
The bigger the bullet, the greater

37
00:02:02,141 --> 00:02:04,940
the wind's effect on its flight.

38
00:02:04,941 --> 00:02:10,740
And the M107 fires a big armor-piercing incendiary round.

39
00:02:10,741 --> 00:02:14,140
This one is a standard API round.

40
00:02:14,141 --> 00:02:16,940
And this will be your M107 round.

41
00:02:16,941 --> 00:02:18,640
The wind will push this bullet all over the place

42
00:02:18,641 --> 00:02:20,940
if you're not careful and know what you're doing with it.

43
00:02:20,941 --> 00:02:23,440
Given the wind, can the sniper team,

44
00:02:23,441 --> 00:02:25,680
using this precisely engineered weapon,

45
00:02:25,681 --> 00:02:30,620
hit three bullseyes more than a quarter mile in the distance?

46
00:02:30,621 --> 00:02:32,240
Target three identified.

47
00:02:32,241 --> 00:02:33,960
437 meters.

48
00:02:33,961 --> 00:02:35,160
437.

49
00:02:35,161 --> 00:02:37,160
Unduxed.

50
00:02:37,161 --> 00:02:39,560
Give me a half right.

51
00:02:39,561 --> 00:02:40,800
Half right.

52
00:02:40,801 --> 00:02:44,320
And send it when you're ready.

53
00:02:44,321 --> 00:02:45,021
Ready.

54
00:02:45,022 --> 00:02:45,722
Send it.

55
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:54,840
Move to the right, V target.

56
00:03:00,440 --> 00:03:02,780
The target on the left, it's the shorter one.

57
00:03:03,020 --> 00:03:03,940
Roger, got shorty.

58
00:03:11,560 --> 00:03:13,520
All right, let's see what happened to the target.

59
00:03:15,680 --> 00:03:19,080
Yes, the M107, devastating.

60
00:03:19,560 --> 00:03:20,860
Punches holes through the target.

61
00:03:20,861 --> 00:03:26,180
This is a one-inch Iron Maiden, and what this round has done, not only has it punched a hole,

62
00:03:26,260 --> 00:03:28,300
but it's melted a hole through this target.

63
00:03:28,780 --> 00:03:32,100
That's why we call it the API, the Armor-Piercing Incendiary Round.

64
00:03:33,020 --> 00:03:36,500
We hit it here in the chest, over here on the side, and up in the head.

65
00:03:37,480 --> 00:03:39,080
Yeah, that person's not having a good day.

66
00:03:39,720 --> 00:03:43,180
This other littler target, well, looks like it was blown off of its braces.

67
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:48,520
We got a solid hole, and then we got a glancing blow there after it fell down.

68
00:03:48,521 --> 00:03:52,680
And if anyone was driving this vehicle, as this poor sucker was,

69
00:03:54,040 --> 00:03:56,420
you can see here where all the black marks are.

70
00:03:56,640 --> 00:03:58,240
These are holes that have been punched through.

71
00:03:58,680 --> 00:04:01,900
Devastating effect of the M107 with an API round.

72
00:04:03,060 --> 00:04:07,980
Once they dial in, these guys can hit bullseyes a quarter mile away all day.

73
00:04:08,460 --> 00:04:11,400
But how about a tank from a range of over half a mile?

74
00:04:12,160 --> 00:04:14,760
They recalibrate for just below the tank's turret.

75
00:04:15,880 --> 00:04:16,720
Send it when you're ready.

76
00:04:18,520 --> 00:04:20,420
bullseye.

77
00:04:25,420 --> 00:04:28,160
Hyper-accurate rifles like the M107

78
00:04:28,161 --> 00:04:33,760
can trace their origins to some pioneering weapons of the past.

79
00:04:36,080 --> 00:04:39,280
The first, says U.S. Army historian David Stushan,

80
00:04:39,940 --> 00:04:42,880
is the Pennsylvania Flintlock from the 1700s.

81
00:04:42,881 --> 00:04:49,880
This is a replica of about a 1760 Flintlock Pennsylvania rifle.

82
00:04:49,881 --> 00:04:55,040
This is a 54 caliber solid soft lead rifle ball.

83
00:04:55,041 --> 00:04:59,280
And it's just like the ones that the less accurate smoothbore muskets fired, but the

84
00:04:59,281 --> 00:05:03,100
difference is that it's wrapped in a greased patch.

85
00:05:03,101 --> 00:05:10,600
This loading block has got balls already patched and greased with sheep tallow and beeswax,

86
00:05:10,601 --> 00:05:13,900
which allows it to load very tightly in the inside of the barrel.

87
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:20,480
So when it's fired, the bullet is spun by the twisted grooves on the inside of the rifle.

88
00:05:21,020 --> 00:05:23,820
What that gives you is greater accuracy and range.

89
00:05:23,980 --> 00:05:27,920
You can shoot a deer or a redcoat at 300 yards.

90
00:05:29,560 --> 00:05:34,200
In its time, the Pennsylvania flintlock rifle was the world's most accurate firearm.

91
00:05:34,940 --> 00:05:36,220
But just how accurate was that?

92
00:05:37,340 --> 00:05:39,300
Dave's target is 25 yards away.

93
00:05:40,600 --> 00:05:42,240
X marks the bullseye.

94
00:05:45,080 --> 00:05:48,820
So the old hunters and marksmen would look where they were aiming

95
00:05:48,821 --> 00:05:50,400
and look where the bullet went,

96
00:05:50,840 --> 00:05:53,400
and they would measure to the opposite direction

97
00:05:53,401 --> 00:05:56,940
to compensate by aiming here so the bullet would hit here.

98
00:05:57,440 --> 00:05:59,460
So this is called Kentucky windage.

99
00:05:59,880 --> 00:06:00,680
Let's try it again.

100
00:06:07,560 --> 00:06:08,760
Not a bullseye,

101
00:06:08,761 --> 00:06:11,520
but plenty close enough to put venison on the table.

102
00:06:13,260 --> 00:06:17,200
In the mid-19th century, the Springfield Model 1842

103
00:06:17,201 --> 00:06:20,320
embodied three new advancements in rifle technology

104
00:06:20,321 --> 00:06:21,760
that made for greater accuracy.

105
00:06:23,840 --> 00:06:26,540
First was a new bullet, the mini-ball.

106
00:06:27,300 --> 00:06:30,060
The difference is it's shaped sort of like a football,

107
00:06:30,420 --> 00:06:32,240
and the base of it is hollow.

108
00:06:32,520 --> 00:06:33,820
It has a cavity or cone.

109
00:06:34,200 --> 00:06:37,880
When it's fired, the powder explodes into this cavity,

110
00:06:37,881 --> 00:06:40,580
making a tight fit or seal inside the barrel.

111
00:06:41,440 --> 00:06:43,880
This, paired with its more aerodynamic shape,

112
00:06:44,520 --> 00:06:46,920
maximized both its accuracy and range.

113
00:06:47,220 --> 00:06:50,100
So instead of having a 300-yard accuracy,

114
00:06:50,520 --> 00:06:52,800
now it's 750 yards.

115
00:06:53,620 --> 00:06:55,220
The Springfield's second advancement

116
00:06:55,221 --> 00:06:57,680
that increased accuracy was an adjustable sight.

117
00:06:58,760 --> 00:07:01,320
It's graduated to every 100 yards

118
00:07:01,321 --> 00:07:03,320
all the way up to 900 yards.

119
00:07:04,420 --> 00:07:06,800
The third improvement was a percussion cap

120
00:07:06,801 --> 00:07:11,400
that ignited the main charge instead of the flint and steel mechanism used in the flintlock.

121
00:07:12,360 --> 00:07:16,600
Not only is this more reliable, but it means that the marksman can have his eyes open

122
00:07:16,601 --> 00:07:20,040
when he's pulling the trigger and not have all those tiny pieces of flint

123
00:07:20,041 --> 00:07:22,780
and that exploding powder in his face as a distraction.

124
00:07:23,720 --> 00:07:27,620
From the same 25 yards, Dave aims again for the bullseye.

125
00:07:28,440 --> 00:07:31,780
Is the Springfield indeed more accurate than the flintlock of a century earlier?

126
00:07:31,781 --> 00:07:39,840
the experts say absolutely but dave's still slightly off

127
00:07:39,841 --> 00:07:46,500
this next rifle should help him out the rugged russian-made mozin nagant

128
00:07:46,501 --> 00:07:53,700
first produced in 1891 it served in various modified forms for 60 years

129
00:07:53,701 --> 00:07:56,540
and became the weapon of choice for soviet snipers

130
00:07:56,541 --> 00:08:03,780
breach loading improved accuracy because now bullets could be made larger than the barrel

131
00:08:03,781 --> 00:08:08,500
and be forced in tightly from the breach rather than rammed loose through the barrel

132
00:08:08,501 --> 00:08:16,680
when equipped with a telescopic sight its accuracy was fearsome can dave finally hit

133
00:08:16,681 --> 00:08:18,520
the bullseye using the more advanced rifle

134
00:08:18,521 --> 00:08:26,520
All in all, that's not bad shooting with these old guns.

135
00:08:27,140 --> 00:08:28,840
But snipers don't shoot at paper.

136
00:08:30,420 --> 00:08:32,500
They shoot watermelons.

137
00:08:36,440 --> 00:08:39,000
It's one thing to make a bullseye out of a watermelon.

138
00:08:41,040 --> 00:08:44,380
When you're a cop, the bullseye can fire back.

139
00:08:44,381 --> 00:08:53,320
Police departments, including the LAPD, have tried to break down fast, accurate and responsible

140
00:08:53,321 --> 00:08:55,320
shooting to a science.

141
00:08:55,321 --> 00:09:02,700
In our presentation to the target, we've broken it down in our teachings to a five-count presentation,

142
00:09:02,701 --> 00:09:04,620
much like a dance step.

143
00:09:04,621 --> 00:09:08,940
Our first count is to maintain our shooting grip and break the safety on the holster.

144
00:09:08,941 --> 00:09:11,980
Our second count is to what we call close contact.

145
00:09:11,981 --> 00:09:14,400
third would be to a low ready.

146
00:09:14,880 --> 00:09:16,660
Count four, align the sights.

147
00:09:16,860 --> 00:09:17,860
And then on count five,

148
00:09:17,900 --> 00:09:19,040
would actually press the trigger.

149
00:09:24,860 --> 00:09:27,500
Rick Bennett has won four world championships

150
00:09:27,501 --> 00:09:29,440
in team police pistol competitions.

151
00:09:31,100 --> 00:09:33,560
A standard target practice distance for Rick

152
00:09:33,561 --> 00:09:36,460
and all police officers is just seven yards,

153
00:09:37,240 --> 00:09:40,120
as most shootouts occur within that narrow range.

154
00:09:40,121 --> 00:09:44,240
What I'm going to do now is post a competition target,

155
00:09:44,820 --> 00:09:48,140
and I'm going to go ahead and shoot that at 25 yards,

156
00:09:48,240 --> 00:09:50,680
which would be a distance that we would engage in competition.

157
00:09:53,040 --> 00:09:55,980
Rick's competition pistol isn't standard police issue.

158
00:09:56,380 --> 00:09:59,480
It's a Colt Model 1911 chambered in 9mm,

159
00:09:59,800 --> 00:10:02,820
and the goal here is to place all six rounds right into the 10 ring.

160
00:10:10,120 --> 00:10:13,120
I'm going to go ahead and give you six more.

161
00:10:24,120 --> 00:10:26,120
You want six more?

162
00:10:26,121 --> 00:10:28,120
We're convinced.

163
00:10:29,120 --> 00:10:35,120
Eons before ballistics, primitive man began our long journey in search of the bullseye with a spear.

164
00:10:35,121 --> 00:10:44,720
about 40,000 years ago a dart throwing weapon called the atlatl followed which used leverage

165
00:10:44,721 --> 00:10:55,760
to improve upon the spear's range and accuracy then 10,000 years ago the earliest bow and arrow

166
00:10:55,761 --> 00:11:02,240
made its mark until the advent of firearms it endured as the most accurate weapon on earth

167
00:11:02,241 --> 00:11:11,440
Today, its high-tech, hyper-accurate incarnation is the compound bow.

168
00:11:11,441 --> 00:11:17,600
Invented for hunting, it's also used in archery competitions.

169
00:11:17,601 --> 00:11:23,040
Unlike traditional bows, it has extra stiff limbs made of composite materials, which must

170
00:11:23,041 --> 00:11:27,240
be bent using a levering system of cables and pulleys.

171
00:11:27,241 --> 00:11:32,200
The key to its accuracy, when fully drawn, the pulleys, not the archer, hold part or

172
00:11:32,201 --> 00:11:34,540
or even most of the draw weight.

173
00:11:34,541 --> 00:11:36,700
This allows the archer to draw and aim the bow

174
00:11:36,701 --> 00:11:38,000
with much less strain.

175
00:11:40,840 --> 00:11:43,440
Levi Morgan and Samantha Klein

176
00:11:43,441 --> 00:11:45,380
are the Archery Shooters Association's

177
00:11:45,381 --> 00:11:49,220
top-ranked male and female shooters for 2008.

178
00:11:49,221 --> 00:11:50,820
We challenged them to hit the bullseye

179
00:11:50,821 --> 00:11:53,420
on the foam targets in the distance.

180
00:11:53,421 --> 00:11:55,120
That's the main thing in our competition,

181
00:11:55,121 --> 00:11:57,260
the unknown distance, and that's what we practice

182
00:11:57,261 --> 00:11:58,460
more than shooting or anything.

183
00:11:58,461 --> 00:12:00,460
We practice judging yardage.

184
00:12:00,461 --> 00:12:03,660
For every yard Levi and Sam misjudge their range,

185
00:12:03,661 --> 00:12:06,220
their arrows will strike one inch high or low

186
00:12:06,221 --> 00:12:10,060
off the bullseye etched on the target's side.

187
00:12:10,061 --> 00:12:13,420
Nowadays, we shoot a scope with a slide bar

188
00:12:13,421 --> 00:12:16,640
where we sight in our own marks, and you just

189
00:12:16,641 --> 00:12:20,260
move it to how far you judge the target for.

190
00:12:20,261 --> 00:12:23,620
They estimate the target's range at 42 yards,

191
00:12:23,621 --> 00:12:26,740
then calibrate their scopes accordingly.

192
00:12:26,741 --> 00:12:29,400
The challenge now is to take perfect aim,

193
00:12:29,401 --> 00:12:31,440
using not only the scope housing,

194
00:12:31,441 --> 00:12:33,920
but also the peep hole mounted on the bow string.

195
00:12:33,921 --> 00:12:36,460
I'm sighting from the peep to my scope.

196
00:12:36,461 --> 00:12:38,680
You want everything to center up on that green fiber.

197
00:12:38,681 --> 00:12:40,160
That has to be in the center of your peep

198
00:12:40,161 --> 00:12:41,920
or you're not going to hit where you want to hit.

199
00:12:41,921 --> 00:12:43,620
When you come full draw, you want that,

200
00:12:43,621 --> 00:12:45,340
the size of the opening in your peep

201
00:12:45,341 --> 00:12:47,600
to match up perfectly with your scope housing.

202
00:12:47,601 --> 00:12:48,960
If you have a bigger hole like this,

203
00:12:48,961 --> 00:12:50,240
it's going to be able to move around in it.

204
00:12:50,241 --> 00:12:52,400
You want the same size so it lines up perfectly.

205
00:12:52,401 --> 00:12:54,300
A lot of people think it's just a matter of pulling back

206
00:12:54,301 --> 00:12:57,060
and letting it go, but something little like that

207
00:12:57,061 --> 00:12:59,400
can change it three or four inches.

208
00:12:59,401 --> 00:13:03,298
the deer. Is their estimate of the range correct?

209
00:13:03,299 --> 00:13:06,400
The only way to find out is to let fly.

210
00:13:22,400 --> 00:13:25,400
And fly some more.

211
00:13:29,400 --> 00:13:36,400
Bullseye precision. Worthy of Robin Hood.

212
00:13:36,401 --> 00:13:42,400
Can these guys be just as accurate with medieval heavy artillery?

213
00:13:42,401 --> 00:13:45,400
Fire in the hole!

214
00:13:45,401 --> 00:13:51,400
An SM-3 missile rises from its sleep on a Navy warship.

215
00:13:51,401 --> 00:13:55,400
It climbs at over 5,000 miles per hour.

216
00:13:55,401 --> 00:13:56,485
miles per hour.

217
00:13:56,486 --> 00:14:02,200
Its mission sounds like something out of science fiction, to strike and destroy

218
00:14:02,201 --> 00:14:11,140
a U.S. satellite falling towards Earth at 17,000 miles per hour. If it succeeds, it

219
00:14:11,141 --> 00:14:15,590
will be like a bullet hitting a bullet. Bullseye.

220
00:14:15,591 --> 00:14:20,040
Astonishing accuracy like this is the culmination

221
00:14:20,041 --> 00:14:23,080
of advances by weapons makers dating back to antiquity.

222
00:14:25,820 --> 00:14:30,480
George Metz is an expert on the ballista,

223
00:14:30,481 --> 00:14:32,780
a torsion-powered projectile launcher,

224
00:14:32,781 --> 00:14:35,860
first used by the Greeks in the fifth century BC,

225
00:14:35,861 --> 00:14:38,020
and later by the Romans.

226
00:14:38,021 --> 00:14:41,660
It fired a short, heavy arrow called a bolt.

227
00:14:41,661 --> 00:14:43,960
I would just look down the barrel, down the slider,

228
00:14:43,961 --> 00:14:46,300
which is like the barrel of a rifle, where I feel it's going

229
00:14:46,301 --> 00:14:47,900
to go, and after a few trial shots,

230
00:14:47,901 --> 00:14:49,340
I'm probably going to be on the mark.

231
00:14:49,341 --> 00:14:50,900
This is an anti-personnel weapon.

232
00:14:51,100 --> 00:14:53,600
It's to fire through an enemy's shield or into his body

233
00:14:53,601 --> 00:14:56,600
at a range of 100, 150 feet with accuracy.

234
00:14:57,700 --> 00:15:00,220
The accuracy of the ballista was limited, however,

235
00:15:00,920 --> 00:15:03,820
as its engineering focused more on power than precision.

236
00:15:04,740 --> 00:15:07,660
But could a skilled warrior overcome its design deficiencies?

237
00:15:09,200 --> 00:15:11,280
George's target is 50 yards away.

238
00:15:12,100 --> 00:15:13,260
It can hit that, yes.

239
00:15:13,920 --> 00:15:16,140
One, two, three.

240
00:15:16,141 --> 00:15:21,100
George compensates by aiming higher.

241
00:15:22,820 --> 00:15:24,000
No more elevation now.

242
00:15:27,940 --> 00:15:31,440
No Roman would give up now, and neither does George.

243
00:15:38,220 --> 00:15:44,880
In the Middle Ages, the search for a more consistently accurate projectile launching weapon culminated with a trebuchet.

244
00:15:46,140 --> 00:15:49,700
Designed to pulverize the walls of a city or castle under siege,

245
00:15:50,220 --> 00:15:53,240
it dominated the landscape of war for over 400 years.

246
00:15:55,140 --> 00:15:58,620
This trebuchet is the handiwork of Grig Mullen and Wayne Neal,

247
00:15:59,380 --> 00:16:01,940
professors of engineering at Virginia Military Institute.

248
00:16:02,780 --> 00:16:04,320
That's got it. Fire in the hole.

249
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:10,820
As a siege weapon, it didn't matter if you were hitting an exact place on the wall

250
00:16:10,821 --> 00:16:13,220
as long as every time you fired you hit the same place.

251
00:16:13,221 --> 00:16:17,460
It didn't matter if you were here or here, as long as every time you fired, you hit here.

252
00:16:18,400 --> 00:16:21,420
A trebuchet works like a seesaw, using leverage.

253
00:16:22,200 --> 00:16:25,540
On one end of a pivoted beam is a sling, holding a projectile.

254
00:16:26,220 --> 00:16:27,860
On the other end is a counterweight.

255
00:16:28,680 --> 00:16:33,040
When the counterweight falls, the beam and sling swing up to a vertical position,

256
00:16:33,680 --> 00:16:35,080
where one end of the sling releases,

257
00:16:35,800 --> 00:16:38,540
propelling the projectile towards the target with great force.

258
00:16:38,541 --> 00:16:45,440
And then by adjusting the sling, they'd hit the top of the wall, start to break the wall

259
00:16:45,441 --> 00:16:49,280
down, and then adjust the sling length and just walk the projectiles down the wall and

260
00:16:49,281 --> 00:16:51,260
knock chunks out of it so that the troops could go in.

261
00:16:52,220 --> 00:16:56,820
We challenged the professors to prove their trebuchet's accuracy by battering this 10

262
00:16:56,821 --> 00:16:59,540
foot by 10 foot target 75 yards away.

263
00:17:00,220 --> 00:17:03,120
The first projectiles in their arsenal, watermelons.

264
00:17:03,400 --> 00:17:06,020
Three, two, one, fire in the hole.

265
00:17:08,540 --> 00:17:17,600
Two variables come into play when adjusting the trebuchet's aim, the weight of the projectile

266
00:17:17,601 --> 00:17:19,060
and the length of the sling.

267
00:17:20,120 --> 00:17:23,480
We're lengthening the sling to make the same weight projectile.

268
00:17:23,540 --> 00:17:27,760
We think we've got another watermelon that's about the same weight release later.

269
00:17:28,140 --> 00:17:29,300
We were going over the target.

270
00:17:29,620 --> 00:17:34,100
By releasing later, it'll be a flatter trajectory and should be more of a line drive into the

271
00:17:34,101 --> 00:17:36,180
target as opposed to a mortar shot over it.

272
00:17:36,181 --> 00:17:37,020
Fire in the hole.

273
00:17:37,021 --> 00:17:41,020
Come on, come on, come on, come on.

274
00:17:41,021 --> 00:17:42,020
Yes!

275
00:17:42,021 --> 00:17:45,020
Yeah, you can see watermelon pieces all over.

276
00:17:45,021 --> 00:17:48,020
That's half-inch plywood, which is not negligible.

277
00:17:48,021 --> 00:17:52,020
And so a 15-pound watermelon at 80 miles an hour

278
00:17:52,021 --> 00:17:54,020
pretty well punched through that.

279
00:17:54,021 --> 00:17:57,020
Normally, they want to hit the wall perpendicular anyway

280
00:17:57,021 --> 00:17:59,020
and not come down on the wall.

281
00:17:59,021 --> 00:18:02,020
Next, we challenge the professors to pummel the bullseye

282
00:18:02,021 --> 00:18:05,020
with a slightly harder projectile,

283
00:18:05,021 --> 00:18:07,120
a 10-pound bowling ball.

284
00:18:12,120 --> 00:18:12,820
Oh.

285
00:18:13,320 --> 00:18:14,560
Like they did for the watermelon,

286
00:18:14,760 --> 00:18:15,860
they'll have to make adjustments

287
00:18:15,861 --> 00:18:17,560
to account for the weight of the bowling ball.

288
00:18:18,700 --> 00:18:21,380
We need to shorten the sling so it releases and goes higher.

289
00:18:21,680 --> 00:18:23,260
We started out positioning the ball

290
00:18:23,261 --> 00:18:24,420
right on the center of the runway,

291
00:18:25,100 --> 00:18:26,120
and now we're shifting it.

292
00:18:26,840 --> 00:18:29,080
Basically, the plane of the sling

293
00:18:29,081 --> 00:18:31,600
perpendicular to the axis is about right here.

294
00:18:31,601 --> 00:18:35,040
So what we've done is shift the plane this way

295
00:18:35,041 --> 00:18:38,540
to get us to where we're throwing slightly to the left.

296
00:18:38,541 --> 00:18:40,400
You can do a little bit of adjusting,

297
00:18:40,401 --> 00:18:42,240
other than moving this whole thing.

298
00:18:42,241 --> 00:18:43,240
Yeah.

299
00:18:44,440 --> 00:18:46,040
Now can they hit the bullseye?

300
00:18:47,420 --> 00:18:49,320
Son of a brick.

301
00:18:49,321 --> 00:18:50,520
We've got distance.

302
00:18:50,521 --> 00:18:52,080
If this was a true siege,

303
00:18:52,081 --> 00:18:54,120
we'd just knock down this section of the wall

304
00:18:54,121 --> 00:18:56,260
instead of this section of the wall.

305
00:18:56,261 --> 00:18:58,800
We can try shifting it even further to the left,

306
00:18:58,801 --> 00:19:00,560
or we can move the wall over.

307
00:19:01,600 --> 00:19:08,040
That's an expedient option medieval trebuchet operators didn't have.

308
00:19:08,041 --> 00:19:13,480
But it's easier to shift a wooden wall than a 4,000-pound trebuchet.

309
00:19:13,481 --> 00:19:18,360
Grieg also pounds in stakes to keep track of where the rounds have hit.

310
00:19:18,361 --> 00:19:26,540
We move the wall and now we hit where the wall used to be.

311
00:19:26,541 --> 00:19:28,540
We need to shift to the right some.

312
00:19:31,540 --> 00:19:34,180
What we've figured out in the years operating these things

313
00:19:34,181 --> 00:19:35,780
is that from the historical record,

314
00:19:35,781 --> 00:19:38,080
we got 95% of the story.

315
00:19:38,081 --> 00:19:40,260
What we're missing is the last 5%

316
00:19:40,261 --> 00:19:43,660
that's the tricks of the trades for a trebuchet operator.

317
00:19:43,661 --> 00:19:46,220
And the last one of those guys died 500 years ago.

318
00:19:46,221 --> 00:19:49,500
When trebuchets went out of use, that knowledge died.

319
00:19:49,501 --> 00:19:51,700
And so the only way we can revive that

320
00:19:51,701 --> 00:19:53,740
is what we're doing now, is practice.

321
00:19:53,741 --> 00:19:55,160
And so we keep trying.

322
00:19:55,161 --> 00:20:02,160
and trying, and trying, and trying again.

323
00:20:02,161 --> 00:20:07,160
Oh, ha, ha, ha, ha!

324
00:20:07,161 --> 00:20:09,160
It smelled burnt rubber.

325
00:20:09,161 --> 00:20:13,160
It hit the wall first, and the friction burned the rubber off the outside of the ball.

326
00:20:13,161 --> 00:20:16,160
It landed right there.

327
00:20:16,161 --> 00:20:19,160
Knocked the stake out that was holding the center of the wall and broke the 2x4,

328
00:20:19,161 --> 00:20:23,160
and we've ruptured the plywood right there.

329
00:20:23,161 --> 00:20:26,160
Although Grig and Wayne never hit the bullseye per se,

330
00:20:26,161 --> 00:20:28,160
they think their aim would have been more than good enough

331
00:20:28,161 --> 00:20:30,160
for a medieval commander.

332
00:20:30,161 --> 00:20:33,160
When we pull the wall out of the way,

333
00:20:33,161 --> 00:20:36,160
you see the stakes where we staked every time we've hit,

334
00:20:36,161 --> 00:20:38,160
you'll see the pattern that we're just right there.

335
00:20:38,161 --> 00:20:40,160
If this was a 20-foot high wall,

336
00:20:40,161 --> 00:20:43,160
we'd have pounded the crud out of it by now.

337
00:20:43,161 --> 00:20:46,160
By the 14th century, the trebuchet was being eclipsed

338
00:20:46,161 --> 00:20:52,160
by a terrifying new long-range weapon, the cannon.

339
00:20:52,161 --> 00:21:02,180
But for 500 years, all cannons had smooth bore barrels in which the projectiles fit loosely, making them clumsy at finding the bullseye.

340
00:21:06,440 --> 00:21:12,200
But the bullseye finally came within reach in the mid-1800s and America's Civil War.

341
00:21:15,100 --> 00:21:21,780
The rifled bore put a new spin on every round fired, revolutionizing the cannon just as it had the musket.

342
00:21:22,160 --> 00:21:28,240
This gave them tremendous accuracy and much harder hitting power.

343
00:21:28,241 --> 00:21:33,160
It also meant that they could now shoot a cannonball further than the gunners or cannoneers

344
00:21:33,161 --> 00:21:34,160
could see.

345
00:21:34,161 --> 00:21:42,040
By World War I, the cannon's longer accurate range had compelled artillery experts to

346
00:21:42,041 --> 00:21:43,780
develop innovative new tactics.

347
00:21:43,781 --> 00:21:50,020
How do you fire over the horizon several miles to an enemy target that the cannon cannot

348
00:21:50,021 --> 00:21:51,520
even see?

349
00:21:51,521 --> 00:21:53,060
And so now you're working with maps,

350
00:21:53,061 --> 00:21:55,460
you're working with graphs and grid lines

351
00:21:55,461 --> 00:21:56,620
and this type of thing.

352
00:21:56,621 --> 00:21:58,660
You have to have communication from the soldiers

353
00:21:58,661 --> 00:22:01,540
who can see the enemy target and tell the cannons

354
00:22:01,541 --> 00:22:04,100
where to shoot and how to adjust their rounds

355
00:22:04,101 --> 00:22:07,100
onto the target to make it more accurate to hit the bullseye.

356
00:22:09,840 --> 00:22:13,180
By the 1940s, artillery experts redefined

357
00:22:13,181 --> 00:22:15,180
the parameters of hitting the bullseye

358
00:22:15,181 --> 00:22:17,880
with a proximity fuse.

359
00:22:17,881 --> 00:22:20,480
Projectiles so equipped no longer had to hit the target

360
00:22:20,481 --> 00:22:24,960
They exploded within a predetermined range of the target.

361
00:22:25,060 --> 00:22:26,860
The fuse is kind of like a radar.

362
00:22:26,960 --> 00:22:28,560
It's got a little radio system in it.

363
00:22:28,660 --> 00:22:32,200
It will emit a signal in the air.

364
00:22:32,300 --> 00:22:35,100
And when it bounces back against a hard object,

365
00:22:35,200 --> 00:22:38,160
like an airplane, it explodes.

366
00:22:40,700 --> 00:22:43,740
Today, the benchmark for bullseye accuracy in war

367
00:22:43,840 --> 00:22:44,840
is the smart bomb.

368
00:22:44,841 --> 00:22:45,740
is the smart bomb.

369
00:22:48,740 --> 00:22:51,840
They use laser guidance from other aircraft,

370
00:22:51,841 --> 00:22:55,720
wingman, to point out and paint the target with a laser signal

371
00:22:55,721 --> 00:23:00,760
so that these bombs would follow that down to the target.

372
00:23:00,761 --> 00:23:04,360
The laser guided munitions were a tremendous quantum leap

373
00:23:04,361 --> 00:23:06,020
forward in guiding munitions.

374
00:23:08,740 --> 00:23:10,400
But they had problems.

375
00:23:10,401 --> 00:23:12,640
For one thing, they couldn't see through clouds,

376
00:23:12,641 --> 00:23:14,900
or at least the observers or those who were aiming them

377
00:23:14,901 --> 00:23:15,960
couldn't see through the clouds.

378
00:23:16,740 --> 00:23:17,440
The solution?

379
00:23:18,080 --> 00:23:21,100
JDAM, short for Joint Direct Attack Munitions.

380
00:23:21,640 --> 00:23:24,060
It's the epitome of ballistic surgical precision.

381
00:23:26,400 --> 00:23:29,760
Even when the smallest of targets is concealed by cloud cover

382
00:23:29,761 --> 00:23:33,760
or the canopy of night, this smart bomb uses GPS

383
00:23:33,761 --> 00:23:38,280
and inertial navigation system monitors to zero in and destroy it.

384
00:23:42,640 --> 00:23:46,480
Hitting the bullseye doesn't always require such high tech.

385
00:23:46,481 --> 00:23:52,480
All this expert needs is a skilled hand and a girl with guts.

386
00:23:52,481 --> 00:23:56,480
David Adamovich, better known as the great Throdini,

387
00:23:56,481 --> 00:23:59,400
is a master of one of the most time-honored and dangerous

388
00:23:59,401 --> 00:24:03,400
tests of bullseye accuracy, knife throwing.

389
00:24:03,401 --> 00:24:06,840
His mastery, however, is to miss.

390
00:24:06,841 --> 00:24:10,400
The entertainment value of his art hinges on David's ability

391
00:24:10,401 --> 00:24:12,440
David's ability to miss his assistant

392
00:24:12,441 --> 00:24:14,100
by just the slimmest of margins.

393
00:24:17,300 --> 00:24:20,740
Slightest error can have dire consequences.

394
00:24:20,741 --> 00:24:24,340
When I'm throwing, that which goes through my head the most

395
00:24:24,341 --> 00:24:28,540
is staying away from the girl and being in my zone.

396
00:24:28,541 --> 00:24:33,580
My target girl is, in essence, an imaginary blur in the zone

397
00:24:33,581 --> 00:24:36,880
that I don't want to throw to versus my focus

398
00:24:36,881 --> 00:24:38,800
on the side of the board where I am throwing.

399
00:24:40,400 --> 00:24:46,380
For greatest accuracy, impalement artists throw each knife end over end, spinning it like a pinwheel.

400
00:24:47,800 --> 00:24:51,480
The number of revolutions increases the farther the thrower is from the target.

401
00:24:52,560 --> 00:24:59,460
From around 7 feet, the shortest standard distance, the knife is held by the blade and makes one half spin.

402
00:25:00,420 --> 00:25:05,300
From about 11 feet, the knife is held by the handle and completes one full spin.

403
00:25:05,301 --> 00:25:11,240
I'm going to show you how I aim to miss at 1 and 1

404
00:25:11,241 --> 00:25:16,080
half spin using target girl Tina.

405
00:25:16,081 --> 00:25:17,740
To successfully complete a 1 and 1

406
00:25:17,741 --> 00:25:23,080
half spin throw, David must move back to a distance of 15 feet.

407
00:25:23,081 --> 00:25:27,260
At this range, the chances for error and the dangers multiply.

408
00:25:32,360 --> 00:25:35,220
David's not just accurate, but speedy.

409
00:25:35,221 --> 00:25:39,360
The Guinness Book of World Records lists him as the world's fastest knife thrower.

410
00:25:40,200 --> 00:25:42,420
Ten knives in 4.2 seconds.

411
00:25:44,740 --> 00:25:46,420
Speed throwing is very difficult.

412
00:25:46,940 --> 00:25:52,000
I'm grabbing knives and throwing knives approximately a quarter second each,

413
00:25:52,160 --> 00:25:55,560
so that each throw is about four-tenths of a second.

414
00:25:55,561 --> 00:25:58,900
So I have to be on my mark perfectly.

415
00:25:59,140 --> 00:26:02,920
I have to grab and throw without ever looking at the knives,

416
00:26:02,921 --> 00:26:05,340
just watching back and forth where I throw the knife.

417
00:26:07,260 --> 00:26:10,260
David's challenge here is to rapidly hurl 10 knives

418
00:26:10,261 --> 00:26:13,580
that will alternate on both the right and left side of his partner's body.

419
00:26:14,900 --> 00:26:17,360
It's called the ladder of death.

420
00:26:25,380 --> 00:26:27,200
I throw anything with a point.

421
00:26:27,780 --> 00:26:31,040
The bigger they are, the further out I have to go.

422
00:26:31,041 --> 00:26:34,580
And when they get big like this and I go that far back,

423
00:26:34,780 --> 00:26:38,660
that is the most dangerous stuff I can do in the impalement darts.

424
00:26:39,460 --> 00:26:43,420
And by all means, it makes me most scared.

425
00:26:44,500 --> 00:26:48,280
Throwing weapons this dangerous magnifies the need for bullseye accuracy.

426
00:26:49,160 --> 00:26:51,640
No one knows that better than Tina, his target girl.

427
00:26:52,040 --> 00:26:56,540
I'm fully aware there exists the possibility of something going wrong.

428
00:26:56,720 --> 00:26:58,360
It's the nature of the act.

429
00:26:58,361 --> 00:27:00,680
You have to commit to it 100%.

430
00:27:00,681 --> 00:27:02,980
It can't be halfway.

431
00:27:03,820 --> 00:27:05,100
Go big or go home.

432
00:27:06,340 --> 00:27:08,640
It's now do or die.

433
00:27:27,120 --> 00:27:30,080
Just when you think things can't get more dangerous,

434
00:27:30,680 --> 00:27:35,340
David has one more test of his bullseye accuracy up his sleeve.

435
00:27:35,820 --> 00:27:39,340
He will try to sever the straw held between Tina's teeth.

436
00:27:48,040 --> 00:27:54,460
There may not be the same element of danger in the game mastered by two-time nine-ball world champion Johnny Archer,

437
00:27:54,461 --> 00:27:56,700
but it requires even more precision

438
00:27:56,701 --> 00:28:00,800
because each shot requires hitting multiple bullseyes

439
00:28:00,801 --> 00:28:05,520
from the cue striking the cue ball in exactly the right spot

440
00:28:05,521 --> 00:28:10,480
to the cue ball halting in an advantageous position

441
00:28:10,481 --> 00:28:11,940
aligned for the next shot.

442
00:28:13,880 --> 00:28:15,180
Johnny makes it all look easy

443
00:28:15,181 --> 00:28:17,340
but he says the hardest shot of all

444
00:28:17,341 --> 00:28:19,700
can be the simple looking straight long shot.

445
00:28:20,260 --> 00:28:22,600
People think, oh, this is going to be an easy shot

446
00:28:22,601 --> 00:28:24,140
because it's long and straight.

447
00:28:24,460 --> 00:28:30,620
The reason I think it's the hardest shot is because you can't miss to hit that cue ball at all on either side.

448
00:28:31,020 --> 00:28:32,040
You're going to miss this ball.

449
00:28:32,041 --> 00:28:40,720
I've got to hit the cue ball dead center in order to make it hit the one ball dead center straight into the hole and miss it.

450
00:28:41,640 --> 00:28:42,760
We're going to do that again.

451
00:28:44,340 --> 00:28:45,640
Told you it was hard.

452
00:28:46,240 --> 00:28:48,440
Well, as you can see, I missed the bullseye.

453
00:28:48,900 --> 00:28:53,040
And the reason I missed it is I hit the cue ball a little bit on the left.

454
00:28:53,041 --> 00:28:58,000
When you're hitting a bullseye, you have to hit your aiming point exactly right.

455
00:29:00,260 --> 00:29:04,160
Making the cue ball settle precisely where you want on any given shot

456
00:29:04,161 --> 00:29:07,340
often requires not striking the cue ball dead center,

457
00:29:07,820 --> 00:29:11,580
but to one side or the other to make it spin or give it English.

458
00:29:14,040 --> 00:29:18,460
Such shots will prove crucial as Johnny takes on the challenge of pocketing every ball

459
00:29:18,461 --> 00:29:21,440
without one miss in a game of nine ball.

460
00:29:23,040 --> 00:29:26,160
The rules call for pocketing the one ball first,

461
00:29:26,560 --> 00:29:28,360
then all the rest in sequential order,

462
00:29:28,800 --> 00:29:30,320
ending with a nine to win the game.

463
00:29:31,520 --> 00:29:33,320
When I look at the table, you know,

464
00:29:33,360 --> 00:29:36,900
what I see is I try to visualize the last ball

465
00:29:36,901 --> 00:29:38,680
I'm going to pocket to win the game,

466
00:29:39,040 --> 00:29:39,980
and I come backwards.

467
00:29:41,220 --> 00:29:42,780
Beginning with his last shot,

468
00:29:43,140 --> 00:29:44,700
Johnny works backwards in his mind.

469
00:29:45,460 --> 00:29:47,900
He mentally plots out the path of the cue ball,

470
00:29:48,440 --> 00:29:50,760
determining where each target ball must be pocketed

471
00:29:50,761 --> 00:29:54,720
and where the cue ball must settle each time to set up the subsequent shot.

472
00:29:57,060 --> 00:30:01,480
In this way, he calculates his all-important first shot for pocketing the one ball.

473
00:30:01,800 --> 00:30:03,400
That will set his strategy in motion.

474
00:30:04,520 --> 00:30:09,300
Now I can see everything that I'm trying to do, and it just looks easy to me.

475
00:30:10,120 --> 00:30:12,000
Easy? Let's see.

476
00:30:12,700 --> 00:30:15,760
Can this world-class player hit every bullseye and run the table?

477
00:30:16,360 --> 00:30:20,380
I'm going to pocket the one ball here, and I want the cue ball in this area

478
00:30:20,381 --> 00:30:21,680
for the angle for the two.

479
00:30:21,880 --> 00:30:23,260
I'm going to hit the cue ball

480
00:30:23,261 --> 00:30:25,200
with a little bit of left-hand English

481
00:30:25,201 --> 00:30:27,200
to have it come out in the center.

482
00:30:28,900 --> 00:30:29,600
Okay?

483
00:30:30,120 --> 00:30:32,060
And now I want to hit the two ball,

484
00:30:32,400 --> 00:30:34,040
send the cue ball off in this angle.

485
00:30:34,420 --> 00:30:36,920
A little bit of left-hand side English

486
00:30:36,921 --> 00:30:37,960
on the cue ball

487
00:30:37,961 --> 00:30:40,000
so I can kind of control it.

488
00:30:42,960 --> 00:30:45,880
I came up a little bit far on the three ball,

489
00:30:46,040 --> 00:30:48,080
but I'm still going to pocket it in the side,

490
00:30:48,081 --> 00:30:51,060
and I want to send a cue ball to the bottom rail,

491
00:30:51,500 --> 00:30:55,140
back out here to this side rail, and in this area here.

492
00:30:55,680 --> 00:30:59,740
So three ball on the side, a little bit of high right-hand English.

493
00:31:01,760 --> 00:31:03,520
As Johnny attacks the four ball,

494
00:31:03,880 --> 00:31:05,960
he demonstrates one of his basic strategies.

495
00:31:06,680 --> 00:31:08,980
Always target an area near the center of the table

496
00:31:08,981 --> 00:31:10,440
for the cue ball to stop.

497
00:31:11,060 --> 00:31:12,460
This increases his options.

498
00:31:15,240 --> 00:31:17,240
Now I have the five in the side,

499
00:31:17,241 --> 00:31:20,920
but I really have to be conscious of my angle on the 6

500
00:31:20,921 --> 00:31:23,340
because the 7 only goes in this pocket.

501
00:31:23,580 --> 00:31:24,960
I got the 5 in the side,

502
00:31:25,100 --> 00:31:28,040
so I have to be able to pocket the 6

503
00:31:28,041 --> 00:31:30,580
and go back up in this area for the 7.

504
00:31:32,240 --> 00:31:34,200
And now I have my angle on the 8,

505
00:31:34,820 --> 00:31:36,480
pocket in the 8 up in that corner.

506
00:31:36,940 --> 00:31:39,420
Now I need to draw it off this side rail

507
00:31:39,421 --> 00:31:41,560
and back down table here,

508
00:31:41,880 --> 00:31:44,080
low left-hand English on this shot.

509
00:31:47,240 --> 00:31:50,080
Now back off the side rail.

510
00:31:50,180 --> 00:31:52,680
Now here's the winning shot.

511
00:31:52,780 --> 00:31:54,640
To make it a little easier for me,

512
00:31:54,740 --> 00:31:57,180
a little bit of high left-hand English.

513
00:31:59,680 --> 00:32:01,180
And that's the winning shot.

514
00:32:01,280 --> 00:32:03,620
You give me a spread like that, I'm going to run out

515
00:32:03,720 --> 00:32:05,060
about 98% of the time.

516
00:32:05,160 --> 00:32:06,920
So that's going to put you in a lot of trouble

517
00:32:07,020 --> 00:32:08,660
if you're playing me.

518
00:32:08,760 --> 00:32:11,760
Johnny Archer's bullseye precision is evident.

519
00:32:11,860 --> 00:32:16,440
But the longest shot he ever takes is nine feet.

520
00:32:16,441 --> 00:32:23,440
Imagine hitting a moving bullseye more than 400 million miles away.

521
00:32:23,441 --> 00:32:29,440
No one has a keener sense of the bullseye than the engineers at NASA.

522
00:32:29,441 --> 00:32:41,440
Mission after mission, they face the challenge of sending spacecraft millions of miles to the smallest of targets at the farthest reaches of our solar system.

523
00:32:41,441 --> 00:32:49,200
Main engine start, 2, 1, 0, and liftoff of the Delta II rocket

524
00:32:49,201 --> 00:32:49,901
with Phoenix.

525
00:32:51,300 --> 00:32:56,020
On August 4, 2007, with the launch of NASA's Phoenix Mars

526
00:32:56,021 --> 00:32:59,540
lander, they took on one of their most daunting tests of precision

527
00:32:59,541 --> 00:33:00,241
navigation.

528
00:33:02,360 --> 00:33:06,440
T plus 130 seconds, and there's jettison,

529
00:33:06,940 --> 00:33:08,160
solid motor jettison.

530
00:33:08,900 --> 00:33:09,600
Their challenge?

531
00:33:09,601 --> 00:33:15,360
send Phoenix on a 10-month voyage of 422 million miles

532
00:33:15,361 --> 00:33:20,800
to a bullseye on Mars just 70 miles long and 15 miles wide.

533
00:33:21,680 --> 00:33:23,180
The odds were against them.

534
00:33:23,900 --> 00:33:27,220
Of the 13 missions that had tried to land probes on Mars previously,

535
00:33:27,840 --> 00:33:29,020
only five had succeeded.

536
00:33:30,540 --> 00:33:34,620
NASA likened the effort to shooting an arrow from Dodger Stadium in Los Angeles,

537
00:33:34,621 --> 00:33:40,440
traveling 2,077 miles and hitting home plate at Wrigley Field in Chicago.

538
00:33:44,320 --> 00:33:47,820
NASA's engineers couldn't simply aim Phoenix directly toward Mars.

539
00:33:50,060 --> 00:33:54,340
Since the red planet orbits the sun at nearly 54,000 miles per hour,

540
00:33:54,900 --> 00:33:56,940
they would have to send their probe toward a point

541
00:33:56,941 --> 00:34:00,600
where they calculated Mars would be 10 months in the future.

542
00:34:00,601 --> 00:34:08,240
If you're shooting skeet, you don't aim where the skeet is now, you aim in front of it because

543
00:34:08,241 --> 00:34:11,051
you're not going to hit it if you shoot where it is now.

544
00:34:11,052 --> 00:34:13,160
You've got to anticipate where it's going.

545
00:34:14,420 --> 00:34:18,720
But engineers knew that the odds of hitting their bullseye on Mars would be astronomical

546
00:34:18,721 --> 00:34:22,240
if they depended solely on the timing and direction of their launch.

547
00:34:24,840 --> 00:34:29,320
That's because an interplanetary probe, surprisingly, is at the mercy of countless

548
00:34:29,321 --> 00:34:32,480
obstacles in the deceptively tranquil vacuum of space.

549
00:34:33,800 --> 00:34:39,340
Launching a spacecraft from Earth and having it get from Earth to Mars is a lot like the

550
00:34:39,341 --> 00:34:40,080
game of golf.

551
00:34:41,340 --> 00:34:44,040
There's wind coming from various directions.

552
00:34:44,660 --> 00:34:47,580
There are trees and sand traps along the way, too.

553
00:34:48,040 --> 00:34:53,860
So in the case of a spacecraft traveling from Earth to Mars, we also have winds to contend

554
00:34:53,861 --> 00:34:54,561
with.

555
00:34:54,562 --> 00:34:58,840
In this case, it's solar winds that affect where the spacecraft is traveling.

556
00:34:59,320 --> 00:35:03,060
There's the gravitational forces of the Earth, the Moon,

557
00:35:04,160 --> 00:35:07,180
the asteroid belt beyond the orbit of Mars,

558
00:35:07,320 --> 00:35:10,580
the large planets, Jupiter, Saturn, the Sun itself,

559
00:35:10,581 --> 00:35:13,960
are forces acting on the spacecraft almost like winds.

560
00:35:14,840 --> 00:35:16,120
To keep Phoenix on target,

561
00:35:16,880 --> 00:35:19,160
NASA's engineers planned several course corrections

562
00:35:19,161 --> 00:35:21,160
at predetermined times during the voyage,

563
00:35:21,800 --> 00:35:23,420
using thrusters on the spacecraft.

564
00:35:24,640 --> 00:35:27,140
Making those corrections required NASA's engineers

565
00:35:27,141 --> 00:35:31,140
to first know Phoenix's precise location, heading, and speed.

566
00:35:31,880 --> 00:35:34,960
They acquired this data from the deep space network of antennas

567
00:35:34,961 --> 00:35:37,880
situated in California, Spain, and Australia,

568
00:35:38,220 --> 00:35:40,820
which was in constant communication with the spacecraft.

569
00:35:42,100 --> 00:35:46,420
On May 17, 2008, 10 months after it departed Earth,

570
00:35:47,720 --> 00:35:50,200
Phoenix sped within 6 million miles of Mars.

571
00:35:52,060 --> 00:35:54,940
NASA executed its fourth and final course correction,

572
00:35:54,941 --> 00:35:58,240
hurling the spacecraft toward a specific point at the fringe

573
00:35:58,340 --> 00:36:00,340
of the Martian atmosphere.

574
00:36:00,440 --> 00:36:02,040
Part of the challenge in navigation

575
00:36:02,140 --> 00:36:05,680
is hitting a very small pinpoint at the top

576
00:36:05,780 --> 00:36:08,620
of the Mars atmosphere to make sure that it actually

577
00:36:08,720 --> 00:36:12,160
achieves the small target on the surface as well.

578
00:36:14,720 --> 00:36:17,060
One week later, Phoenix successfully

579
00:36:17,160 --> 00:36:19,760
hit its mark above Mars.

580
00:36:19,860 --> 00:36:22,240
But a final crucial challenge remained.

581
00:36:22,340 --> 00:36:24,940
Entry, descent, and landing, or two

582
00:36:24,941 --> 00:36:25,940
EDL.

583
00:36:25,941 --> 00:36:28,940
The main purpose of EDL is to take a spacecraft that is

584
00:36:28,941 --> 00:36:32,940
traveling at 12,500 miles an hour relative to the surface and

585
00:36:32,941 --> 00:36:37,940
bring it to a screeching halt in a soft way in a very short

586
00:36:37,941 --> 00:36:39,940
amount of time.

587
00:36:39,941 --> 00:36:43,940
Failure now would lay waste to a $457 million project

588
00:36:43,941 --> 00:36:47,940
to study the history of water on Mars.

589
00:36:47,941 --> 00:36:49,940
We have to make sure that when we hit the atmosphere, we're

590
00:36:49,941 --> 00:36:52,940
hitting it at the right speed and we're hitting it at the

591
00:36:52,941 --> 00:36:54,900
And we're hitting it at the right angle.

592
00:36:54,901 --> 00:36:57,840
If you come in too steep, you can burn up.

593
00:36:57,841 --> 00:37:00,620
If you come in too shallow, you can actually

594
00:37:00,621 --> 00:37:03,480
kind of go dip into the atmosphere and then skip out,

595
00:37:03,481 --> 00:37:06,560
and then not land on the planet at all.

596
00:37:06,561 --> 00:37:10,140
Phoenix now one minute past the entry point.

597
00:37:10,141 --> 00:37:13,120
Could Phoenix complete its 422 million mile

598
00:37:13,121 --> 00:37:18,040
odyssey and hit its target zone on the frozen plain below?

599
00:37:18,041 --> 00:37:20,560
Phoenix deployed its parachute, standing by,

600
00:37:20,561 --> 00:37:21,440
telemetry acquisition.

601
00:37:22,940 --> 00:37:25,440
Could the arrow fired from Dodger Stadium

602
00:37:25,540 --> 00:37:27,940
strike home plate at Wrigley Field?

603
00:37:28,040 --> 00:37:29,340
The separation detected.

604
00:37:29,440 --> 00:37:31,940
We have required the signal.

605
00:37:32,040 --> 00:37:34,180
Gravity turn detected.

606
00:37:34,280 --> 00:37:36,680
Altitude 600 meters.

607
00:37:36,780 --> 00:37:38,880
250 meters.

608
00:37:38,980 --> 00:37:41,000
100 meters.

609
00:37:41,100 --> 00:37:43,000
50 meters standing back for touchdown.

610
00:37:46,100 --> 00:37:47,440
Touchdown signal detected.

611
00:37:50,740 --> 00:37:51,740
Bullseye.

612
00:37:51,741 --> 00:37:59,680
Back on Earth, the challenge for this bunch of High Flyers is to hit multiple bullseyes

613
00:37:59,681 --> 00:38:06,180
using themselves as projectiles.

614
00:38:06,181 --> 00:38:10,500
The challenge of hitting the bullseye enters a whole new dimension when the projectile isn't

615
00:38:10,501 --> 00:38:19,600
a bullet, or billiard ball, or spacecraft, but a human being.

616
00:38:19,601 --> 00:38:25,080
No one knows this better than these precise and daring artists preparing for showtime.

617
00:38:25,081 --> 00:38:32,420
The cast of Cirque du Soleil's acrobatic production, Mystère, in Las Vegas.

618
00:38:32,421 --> 00:38:37,560
The centerpiece of one of their most breathtaking routines is a teeter-totter-like device called

619
00:38:37,561 --> 00:38:40,100
the Korean plank.

620
00:38:40,101 --> 00:38:47,820
Their bullseye is the 14-inch by 14-inch square at the plank's end.

621
00:38:47,821 --> 00:38:54,820
The artists alternate roles as pushers, those who strike the plank, and jumpers, those who

622
00:38:54,821 --> 00:38:59,960
are propelled into the air.

623
00:38:59,961 --> 00:39:04,520
Not only must their aim be perfect, but also their timing.

624
00:39:04,521 --> 00:39:07,240
If they lose their timing, bad things can happen.

625
00:39:07,241 --> 00:39:13,580
If a jumper jumps just a little bit late, the pusher feels that the person is very heavy

626
00:39:13,581 --> 00:39:15,740
on the other side of the board, and most probably,

627
00:39:15,741 --> 00:39:19,020
they will get a buckle or a buckaroo.

628
00:39:19,021 --> 00:39:23,180
It's not a very technical term, but it's a fall, basically,

629
00:39:23,181 --> 00:39:25,900
which could injure them and take them out of the show

630
00:39:25,901 --> 00:39:26,900
for a few days.

631
00:39:26,901 --> 00:39:29,760
If they jump early, now the pusher who's coming

632
00:39:29,761 --> 00:39:33,200
from the other side is landing on an empty board

633
00:39:33,201 --> 00:39:37,500
and hits the floor really hard and can get injured.

634
00:39:37,501 --> 00:39:39,400
So there's injuries that can happen

635
00:39:39,401 --> 00:39:41,940
on both sides of the board, so they must do this

636
00:39:41,941 --> 00:39:43,600
extremely well timed.

637
00:39:44,540 --> 00:39:47,620
Each acrobat must not only time each move perfectly,

638
00:39:49,620 --> 00:39:52,480
but must also trust that his partner will do the same.

639
00:39:53,600 --> 00:39:55,940
Without the trust, there's no way to work.

640
00:39:56,160 --> 00:40:00,560
They build that trust where they can now blindly jump with each other

641
00:40:00,561 --> 00:40:02,960
and throw very complicated tricks,

642
00:40:03,340 --> 00:40:06,360
knowing that they will get the proper push

643
00:40:06,361 --> 00:40:09,140
and they will land on a board that's not empty.

644
00:40:09,520 --> 00:40:11,240
Even though they don't know what's going on,

645
00:40:11,241 --> 00:40:13,500
when they're in the air and flipping and turning,

646
00:40:13,840 --> 00:40:16,380
but they just trust that the partner is there

647
00:40:16,381 --> 00:40:18,440
because they've built that trust over years.

648
00:40:20,460 --> 00:40:22,960
This trust extends to the troop's spotters,

649
00:40:23,480 --> 00:40:25,340
standing ready during the entire routine.

650
00:40:26,900 --> 00:40:29,380
It's their job to help keep the jumpers on target

651
00:40:29,381 --> 00:40:34,000
and act as a safety net if a bullseye is missed.

652
00:40:34,640 --> 00:40:38,160
I have to read my body language from the top and move here.

653
00:40:38,161 --> 00:40:41,300
Because from this position, I'll fall.

654
00:40:41,660 --> 00:40:44,240
On the board, the white mark, this is for all of us.

655
00:40:44,380 --> 00:40:46,440
It's kind of different colors you can see,

656
00:40:46,740 --> 00:40:48,400
so we can see where to put our feet.

657
00:40:48,820 --> 00:40:49,900
We don't have too much space.

658
00:40:52,100 --> 00:40:54,060
For every jumper after the takeoff,

659
00:40:54,380 --> 00:40:57,340
it's not enough to simply hit the plank perfectly on his descent.

660
00:40:59,700 --> 00:41:02,400
Unlike a bullet that needs only to travel to its target,

661
00:41:03,460 --> 00:41:05,720
these human projectiles have to do it

662
00:41:05,721 --> 00:41:07,680
with breathtaking grace and style.

663
00:41:08,160 --> 00:41:12,660
You jump higher and higher, straighter and straighter,

664
00:41:12,661 --> 00:41:16,560
and you learn basic flipping techniques,

665
00:41:16,561 --> 00:41:20,600
simple saltos forward, backwards, twisting.

666
00:41:20,601 --> 00:41:24,200
And then later on, you start to combine those,

667
00:41:24,201 --> 00:41:28,820
flipping and twisting, multiple flipping and twisting,

668
00:41:28,821 --> 00:41:33,160
and multiple twisting and multiple flipping.

669
00:41:33,161 --> 00:41:36,160
It's showtime.

670
00:41:36,161 --> 00:41:39,540
During the five-minute routine, the team's challenge

671
00:41:39,541 --> 00:41:43,460
is to flawlessly hit the bullseye on the plank 58 times.

672
00:41:46,940 --> 00:41:49,660
One error could not only spoil the performance,

673
00:41:50,220 --> 00:41:52,120
but result in serious injury.

674
00:41:53,380 --> 00:41:56,160
But tonight, they're perfect.

675
00:42:01,260 --> 00:42:04,560
These masters of precision make it all look effortless.

676
00:42:04,561 --> 00:42:11,360
But hitting the bullseye in all of its various forms

677
00:42:11,460 --> 00:42:16,000
will always challenge and tantalize us.

678
00:42:16,100 --> 00:42:17,900
We constantly seek perfection.

679
00:42:20,500 --> 00:42:23,820
A bullseye defines it.

